speechtext:
On motion of Hon. C. C. BALLANTYNE (Minister of Marine and Fisheries) the House went into Committee on the following proposed resolution, Mr. Boivin in the Chair.
Resolved, That it is expedient to amend the Acts respecting the appointment of a Harbour Master at the Port of Halifax, chapter forty-nine of the Statutes of 1882, and chapter seventy-eight of the Statutes of 1885, by providing :-
1. That the Harbour Master of the Port of Halifax shall be remunerated for his services solely by the fees, or the portion of the fees, which he may be authorized, by the rules and regulations, to collect in respect of all ships over twenty tons register, entering the Port of Halifax, but which shall not at any time exceed the following rates:-
(a)	For every ship over twenty tons and not over fifty tons register, fifty cents;
(b)	For every ship over fifty tons and not over one hundred tons register, one dollar;
(c)	For every ship over one hundred tons and not over two hundred tons register, one dollar and fifty cents;
(d)	For every ship over two hundred tons and not over three hundred tons register, two dollars;
(e)	For every ship over three hundred tons and not over four hundred tons register, two dollars and fifty cents;
(f)	For every ship over four hundred tons and not over five hundred tons register, three dollars;
(g)	For every ship over five hundred tons and not over seven hundred tons register, four dollars:
(h)	For every ship over seven hundred tons and not over one thousand tons register, five dollars;

(i)	For every ship over one thousand tons register, seven dollars.
Ships of twenty tons register and under not to be subject to any duty under this resolution, nor ships engaged in trading or plying between ports and places in the Dominion of Canada.
2. That the Harbour Master of the Port of Halifax may. out of the moneys received by him for fees, retain for his own remuneration such sum. not exceeding three thousand dollars per annum, as the Governor in Council may authorize ; and if the moneys received by him for fees in any calendar year amount to a less sum than that allowed him by the Governor in Council, then such sum shall be his remuneration for that year.
Mr. BALLA.NTYNE: The object of this resolution is to enable the Government to pay a reasonable salary to the harbour master at Halifax, having regard to present-day conditions. In 1885 the salary was fixed at $1,800 a year, but out of this he was obliged to pay boat hire and the expenses of his office. It can scarcely be said that such remuneration for an officer looking after a harbour of the importance of Halifax is sufficient. If this salary was considered reasonable thirty-four years ago it is manifestly inadequate to-day. The resolution is to provide the means of putting the matter right. The resolution also deals with the fee collectable on vessels of over one thousand tons registered tonnage entering the harbour, the amount being increased from $5 to $7 per ton. This will add greatly to the revenue, which in ordinary times is scarcely sufficient to make ends meet. The resolution, which involves the expenditure of money, has been duly recommended by His Excellency the Governor General. The total fees collected in Halifax from 1914 to 1918 were as follows: 1914, $1,822.50; 1915, $1,714; 1916, $1,783.50; 1917, $1,832; 1918, $3,351.
The large sum collected in 1918 was duo almost entirely to the war, and to so many vessels entering the port of Halifax. The object of this Bill is to enable the harbour master to get a larger remuneration than $1,800, but in no event can his salary be more than the total amount of the fees he collects. As a comparison I might take some of the other ports where the harbour masters are not paid by fees. The harbour master of the port of Montreal receives $5,000; of Quebec, $3,500; of Vancouver, $1,800, and fees as shipping master; of Three Rivers-the amount is not inserted, but I know from memory that it is $900 a year. Two' important matters are embodied in this Bill. One is to enable the harbour master to draw more than $1,800 a year, provided the fees exceed that amount; and the other is to increase the
fees of vessels over 1,000 tons register from $5 to $7.
